WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.069
-We were lucky enough
to have you on our show twice

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.704
while you were
in the White House.

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:07.307
The first time was 2012
on "Late Night."

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:11.811
And then again in 2016,
at the end of your presidency.

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:13.979
And I have to say that I saw
a different

00:00:13.979 --> 00:00:16.716
President Obama
the second time around.

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:18.818
Because the first time around --

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:20.887
I don't know if you
remember this, but we --

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.922
we did a "Slow Jam
the News" bit.

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:25.492
And at the end, I go --
and it was great.

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:27.626
It was one of our best things
we ever did.

00:00:27.626 --> 00:00:31.997
And I said, "At the end,
can you go like, 'Ohh, yeaah'?"

00:00:31.997 --> 00:00:33.733
And you go, "Uh, no."

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:35.200
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.303
-I go, "Yeah, but, maybe, can
you give like a little --

00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:38.905
just a little like a ohhh."

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.572
And you go, "Yeah, no,
I'm not going to do that.

00:00:40.572 --> 00:00:41.875
I'll say, 'Oh, yeah.'"

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:44.143
And I go,
"Okay, yep, absolutely right."

00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:46.846
And by the way, those writers,
Bashir and Diallo,

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:48.347
went on to do
"Sherman's Showcase"

00:00:48.347 --> 00:00:50.316
and "South Side."
They're doing really well.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:53.419
Like, the same writers
that wrote "Slow Jam."

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:55.187
And they were so excited
to write that for ya.

00:00:55.187 --> 00:00:58.490
-That was -- one of my greatest
pieces ever, by the way.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:00:59.659
-Thank you.
-I -- I loved --

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:01.160
I loved "Slow Jam."

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:02.261
-Thank you.
I appreciate that.

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:03.863
-That was terrific.
-That was fun.

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:06.298
And then, the end of
your presidency,

00:01:06.298 --> 00:01:07.900
we did another "Slow Jam."

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.770
And at the end, I wrote in
a part where I wanted you to,

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:15.175
like, kind of stiffly say,
"Like Rihanna,

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:18.644
we have to go to work,
work, work, work, work."

00:01:18.644 --> 00:01:20.946
And you go, "Why do you have me
doing that?

00:01:20.946 --> 00:01:23.550
Like, can't I go like,
work, work, work, work, work"?

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.718
and I go "Stop. You cannot."

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:27.153
And I was telling you,
no, no, no.

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:29.321
You're this close
to leaving the White House.

00:01:29.321 --> 00:01:32.124
You're not --
do not do that now.

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:33.893
You've been so perfect
all the way.

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:35.528
No, do not --
we're not doing that.

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:36.862
And you go, "Okay. Alright.
Yeah."

00:01:36.862 --> 00:01:39.932
-Are you saying that that would
have affected my legacy?

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:42.935
Because that is a jam
and I know that jam well.

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:45.904
-[ Laughs ] No.

00:01:45.904 --> 00:01:48.641
-So, I'm a little insulted...
-Yeah, you should be.

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:50.110
-...that you didn't think
I could pull it off.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.077
-You should be.
But I saved your legacy.

00:01:52.077 --> 00:01:54.314
That moment in time and,
hopefully,

00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:57.216
that makes it in
the second volume of the book.

00:01:57.216 --> 00:01:59.184
That night, I also
introduced you to Madonna.

00:01:59.184 --> 00:02:00.519
Do you remember that?

00:02:00.519 --> 00:02:03.122
-Yes, I do remember that.
-And I will never forget this

00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:05.224
because I go, "Madonna would
like to meet you."

00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:06.993
And you go, "Sure."

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:08.694
And we went over to see Madonna
and she --

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:10.563
you know, she's so, like, tiny.

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:13.365
But she's, like, very like --
she's very flirty

00:02:13.365 --> 00:02:16.936
and kind of just very,
like, you know, she's Madonna.

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:18.004
She's very sexy.

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:21.006
And she was like, "Hello, hi."

00:02:21.006 --> 00:02:22.808
And you go, all business,
you're like,

00:02:22.808 --> 00:02:24.944
"Hello. Pleasure to meet you."

00:02:24.944 --> 00:02:26.679
I was like, "That's right.
That's the way --

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:28.714
Michelle would be like, 'That's
right. That's how you do it.'"

00:02:28.714 --> 00:02:30.016
-You know what?

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:35.087
Don't think that
you elected a fool.

00:02:35.087 --> 00:02:37.189
-[ Laughing ] Okay.
-I knew.

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:39.058
-Yep, you knew.

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:41.027
-I knew how
to handle my business.

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:44.097
-[ Laughing ] Yeah, exactly.

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:47.367
We talked about music,
and you were saying

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:49.735
that you were like
an Earth, Wind, and Fire guy.

00:02:49.735 --> 00:02:52.872
And then, you were
talking about Dylan.

00:02:52.872 --> 00:02:55.608
There's so many
iconic music moments

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.143
in your White House history,

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:58.611
and you talk about it
in the book.

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:03.782
And you'll even have a Spotify
playlist based on the book.

00:03:03.782 --> 00:03:07.186
I just thought we'd talk
about a couple memories.

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:10.657
Beyoncé, "At Last."

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:14.761
-Beyoncé sang --
She sang at our inauguration.

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:19.265
That was our first dance with --

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:24.770
at the very first ball,
and it was spectacular.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.273
-I mean, her doing "At Last" --
And Michelle was dressed in

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:30.709
that white gown,
and as I write in the book,

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:36.148
you know, she was
just...special.

00:03:36.148 --> 00:03:37.683
-Yeah.
-Beautiful.

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:40.686
-Yep, just stunning.
-Yeah.

00:03:40.686 --> 00:03:44.257
-Bob Dylan -- I don't really
remember the story.

00:03:44.257 --> 00:03:47.025
Did he not talk
to you or something?

00:03:47.025 --> 00:03:48.694
-That's what happened.

00:03:48.694 --> 00:03:49.828
He was Dylan-esque.

00:03:49.828 --> 00:03:51.831
He's exactly how
you wanted him to be.

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:54.167
-Yes!
-So, you know, we --

00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:57.669
I describe in the book,
we have these concerts.

00:03:57.669 --> 00:04:00.406
And they were really wonderful.

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:01.641
They'd be themed.

00:04:01.641 --> 00:04:03.408
You know, so you had
a Motown night or you had

00:04:03.408 --> 00:04:06.879
a, you know, Broadway.

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:10.717
-Yep.
-Or we had a poetry slam.

00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:14.387
And, you know,
we'd usually have kids

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:17.223
from the surrounding area
come in,

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:20.526
and the artists would
do workshops on music

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:22.861
or performance.

00:04:22.861 --> 00:04:24.663
And then, they'd do rehearsals.

00:04:24.663 --> 00:04:27.333
And so, by the time
you actually had the concert,

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.068
you know, they'd been
hanging around for a while.

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:30.837
There was a photo line.

00:04:30.837 --> 00:04:33.506
Dylan's -- he skips all that,
right?

00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:35.675
Literally shows up a few minutes

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:37.376
before he's about to go on.

00:04:37.376 --> 00:04:39.412
Comes on, on his trio.

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:41.514
Does this incredible rendition

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:44.316
of "The Times
They Are A-Changin'."

00:04:44.316 --> 00:04:45.851
-Gosh.
-Beautiful.

00:04:45.851 --> 00:04:47.887
-Yep.
-Finishes.

00:04:47.887 --> 00:04:50.189
Walks off the stage.

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:53.025
Stands in front of Michelle
and me.

00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:56.228
Does a quick nod, leaves.
-He's out.

00:04:56.228 --> 00:04:57.596
-That's it.
-He's gone.

00:04:57.596 --> 00:04:59.966
-And just mysterious, you know,
has a little

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:02.501
kind of quirky smile
on his face.

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:04.370
-Yep.
-Perfect.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.072
-Exactly what you want
from Bob Dylan.

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:07.874
-That's what you want
from Bob Dylan.

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:10.609
You know, you don't want him
to be all, you know,

00:05:10.609 --> 00:05:13.045
chatty and, you know, eating --

00:05:13.045 --> 00:05:14.714
eating off the cheese plate
and all that stuff, right?

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:16.049
-It wouldn't be real.
It wouldn't be him.

00:05:16.049 --> 00:05:17.383
-It'd disappoint ya.
-Exactly right.

00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:18.551
-Exactly.

00:05:18.551 --> 00:05:21.854
-Where does your love
for music come from?

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:24.456
-Well, look. I don't --

00:05:24.456 --> 00:05:28.528
I don't come
from a musical family,

00:05:28.528 --> 00:05:35.435
but I think that partly because
I had such a strange childhood,

00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:36.802
right?

00:05:36.802 --> 00:05:40.673
Mom from Kansas, Dad from Kenya,
in Hawaii,

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:42.641
lives in Indonesia for a while,
et cetera.

00:05:42.641 --> 00:05:45.277
-That's an interesting playlist
right there, yeah.

00:05:45.277 --> 00:05:46.812
-Yeah. Well, exactly.

00:05:46.812 --> 00:05:49.515
But what it also meant was

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:52.818
I was moving around a lot.

00:05:52.818 --> 00:05:55.855
I was an only child
till I was nine.

00:05:55.855 --> 00:06:02.028
And so I think music
becomes one of those things

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:04.029
that keeps you company.

00:06:04.029 --> 00:06:08.467
And it becomes a way for you
to connect with other people

00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:10.203
and kids your age.

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:12.738
And so, you know,
I still remember

00:06:12.738 --> 00:06:15.407
the first two albums I ever
bought with my own money, right,

00:06:15.407 --> 00:06:16.942
Stevie Wonder's "Talking Book"

00:06:16.942 --> 00:06:20.112
and Elton John's
"Goodbye Yellow Brick Road."

00:06:20.112 --> 00:06:22.448
-Ah, phew.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:24.583
-Pretty good choices.
-Yeah.

00:06:24.583 --> 00:06:27.085
-I have to say.
-Fantastic ones.

00:06:27.085 --> 00:06:29.756
-Yeah, and, you know,
from that time forward,

00:06:29.756 --> 00:06:33.325
I think music was just always,
you know,

00:06:33.325 --> 00:06:34.793
something I cared deeply about.

00:06:34.793 --> 00:06:40.232
And Michelle and I,
as I describe in the book,

00:06:40.232 --> 00:06:45.871
I think used music as a way
of reminding people of --

00:06:45.871 --> 00:06:48.073
the magic of America is

00:06:48.073 --> 00:06:50.976
we have all these traditions
that we draw from,

00:06:50.976 --> 00:06:52.878
that all get kind of jumbled up,
right?

00:06:52.878 --> 00:06:58.517
You know, you got country music
and blues and rock 'n' roll

00:06:58.517 --> 00:07:04.223
and gospel and hip-hop
and, you know, reggaeton.

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:07.693
And all that stuff is
this medley,

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:11.163
this blend of, you know,
Irish folk songs

00:07:11.163 --> 00:07:16.035
and African drums and, you know,
all these different traditions.

00:07:16.035 --> 00:07:22.441
And that, in some ways, is what
makes America exceptional.

00:07:22.441 --> 00:07:25.478
It's the reason why American
culture exports everywhere.

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:30.082
Because people get a sense of
"There's a piece of me in here."

00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:31.984
-Yeah.
-And so we thought it was

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:34.252
important to have that kind of
music in the White House

00:07:34.252 --> 00:07:37.223
as representing the kind of
America we should want,

00:07:37.223 --> 00:07:39.958
where everybody's, you know,
part of the band.

00:07:39.958 --> 00:07:42.294
-Yeah.
-And everybody, you know,

00:07:42.294 --> 00:07:46.365
gets to play and sing
and make music.

00:07:46.365 --> 00:07:49.202
-I agree with that because
when I was growing up,

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:50.736
it was either like
what are you into?

00:07:50.736 --> 00:07:52.771
Are you into rock?
Are you into pop?

00:07:52.771 --> 00:07:54.373
Are you into classical?

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:57.576
It's like, there -- now, it's
the -- this new iPod generation.

00:07:57.576 --> 00:08:00.779
Or, you know, and where
it's just like --

00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:02.248
-Yeah.
It's an example of --

00:08:02.248 --> 00:08:05.217
of what I meant
when I said these kids --

00:08:05.217 --> 00:08:06.752
you know,
they're better than us.

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:09.288
They're -- because they're
drawing from everything, right?

00:08:09.288 --> 00:08:11.290
-Yeah, legitimate --
like, honestly.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.425
-They don't think
in terms of categories.

00:08:13.425 --> 00:08:17.597
You know, sometimes Sasha,
you know, she's got, you know,

00:08:17.597 --> 00:08:19.432
2 Chainz on and, then
the next thing you know,

00:08:19.432 --> 00:08:20.732
she's got an Elvis song on.

00:08:20.732 --> 00:08:25.404
And then suddenly,
it's, you know, Drake,

00:08:25.404 --> 00:08:30.175
and, next thing you know,
it's, you know,

00:08:30.175 --> 00:08:32.378
some Rachmaninoff or something,
and like,

00:08:32.378 --> 00:08:33.712
"What are you doing?"
-Yeah.

00:08:33.712 --> 00:08:35.647
-But, to them,
it all makes sense.

00:08:35.647 --> 00:08:38.384
And I think that's an indicator

00:08:38.384 --> 00:08:44.022
of how their hearts and minds

00:08:44.022 --> 00:08:49.394
are more embracing of
differences

00:08:49.394 --> 00:08:53.064
and see the good
in so many people.

00:08:53.064 --> 00:08:56.402
-Speaking of Drake,
he recently said that he'd love

00:08:56.402 --> 00:08:58.304
to play you in a movie.

00:08:58.304 --> 00:09:01.540
And you gave your stamp
of approval.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:03.208
What would your advice

00:09:03.208 --> 00:09:06.212
would you give Drake in order
to nail the performance?

00:09:06.212 --> 00:09:08.214
What's the key to playing
Barack Obama?

00:09:08.214 --> 00:09:10.949
-Well, apparently,
based on all the people

00:09:10.949 --> 00:09:11.984
who do imitations of me,

00:09:11.984 --> 00:09:16.221
the key is to
talk really slowly.

00:09:16.221 --> 00:09:18.524
-Why would anyone do that?
That is offensive.

00:09:18.524 --> 00:09:22.028
-And the...slower...

00:09:22.028 --> 00:09:23.328
-[ Laughs ]

00:09:23.328 --> 00:09:25.031
-...the more...

00:09:25.031 --> 00:09:28.100
-Oh, my --
-...strange pauses there are...

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:29.835
-[ Laughs ]

00:09:29.835 --> 00:09:31.703
This is what the audio --
-...in your speech...

00:09:31.703 --> 00:09:33.338
-This is what the audio book
sounds like.

00:09:33.338 --> 00:09:34.473
This is unbelievable.

00:09:34.473 --> 00:09:36.408
[ Laughter ]

00:09:36.408 --> 00:09:40.479
The audio book is actually
365 days long.

00:09:40.479 --> 00:09:43.415
It is living a year
with you talking.

00:09:43.415 --> 00:09:45.484
It is so great talking to you.

00:09:45.484 --> 00:09:48.620
Congratulations, again,
on "A Promised Land."

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:50.322
I really love speaking
with you,

00:09:50.322 --> 00:09:53.726
and I meant it when I said it.
I miss you.

